+# Agent Instructions
+
+This project uses Shipshape, a context-isolated spec-driven workflow for coding agents.
+
+Agent opening this project: ensure Shipshape is installed, then load the `shipshape` skill (`shipshape:shipshape` under the plugin channel) and follow its routing before other work. Decide how to involve the human per your configured preferences.
+
+Tooling values such as stack, directories, and commands live in `RIGGING.md`.
+
+## Project summary
+
+TodoMVC app: vanilla JS, no preprocessors. Tests run via Cucumber with happy-dom for browser simulation. The harness loads the real `index.html` and `js/app.js` into a happy-dom window before every scenario.
+
+## Verification support
+
+`features/support/world.js` sets up the happy-dom window, restores localStorage across scenarios, dispatches lifecycle events, and routes hash navigation. Every scenario exercises the real production code; no mocks or fakes.
+
+## Key files
+
+- `js/app.js` — application logic (render, CRUD, routing, persistence)
+- `css/app.css` — application styles
+- `index.html` — page markup
+- `features/todos.feature` — core TodoMVC behaviour specs
+- `features/rendering.feature` — DOM identity preservation spec
+- `features/edit-conformance.feature` — edit handler guard conformance
+- `features/filter-routing.feature` — hash-based filter routing (@captain)
+- `features/persistence.feature` — localStorage persistence (@captain)
+- `features/methodology-conformance.feature` — methodology checks (@conformance)
+- `assets/app-spec.md` — TodoMVC application specification
+- `.shipshape/plank-join.js` — plank conformance checker
+
+## Known issues
+
+The edit handler has a browser-only double-fire defect: both the Enter keydown handler and the blur handler (capture phase) call `editTodo` and `render`. In a real browser, removing a focused `<li>` via `removeChild` fires `blur` synchronously, causing nested renders. The happy-dom harness does not reproduce this. See `features/edit-conformance.feature` for the conformance guard.
